## De Rham Cohomology of $\mathbb{CP}^n$

**Claim.** For $0 \le k \le 2n$,
$$H^k_{\mathrm{dR}}(\mathbb{CP}^n) \cong \begin{cases} \mathbb{R}, & k \text{ even},\; 0 \le k \le 2n,\\ 0, & k \text{ odd}.\end{cases}$$

---

### Proof

We use the standard cell decomposition of $\mathbb{CP}^n$ together with a comparison between cellular and de Rham cohomology, and we verify the result independently via the Kähler form.

**Step 1: CW structure of $\mathbb{CP}^n$.** Write $\mathbb{CP}^n$ as the quotient of $\mathbb{C}^{n+1}\setminus\{0\}$ by $\mathbb{C}^*$. In homogeneous coordinates $[z_0:\cdots:z_n]$, set
$$U_j = \{[z_0:\cdots:z_n] \in \mathbb{CP}^n : z_j \ne 0\}, \qquad j=0,\dots,n.$$
Each $U_j \cong \mathbb{C}^n$ is an open cell. The closed cells are obtained by the filtration
$$\mathbb{CP}^0 \subset \mathbb{CP}^1 \subset \cdots \subset \mathbb{CP}^n,$$
where $\mathbb{CP}^j \setminus \mathbb{CP}^{j-1} \cong \mathbb{C}^j \cong \mathbb{R}^{2j}$ is an open cell of (real) dimension $2j$. Thus $\mathbb{CP}^n$ has exactly one cell in each even dimension $0, 2, 4, \dots, 2n$, and no odd-dimensional cells.

**Step 2: Cellular chain complex.** The cellular chain group $C_k$ is $\mathbb{R}$ when $k \in \{0,2,4,\dots,2n\}$ and $0$ otherwise. Since there are no two consecutive nonzero chain groups, every cellular boundary map is automatically zero. Hence
$$H_k^{\mathrm{cell}}(\mathbb{CP}^n;\mathbb{R}) \cong \begin{cases} \mathbb{R}, & k \in \{0,2,4,\dots,2n\},\\ 0, & \text{otherwise}.\end{cases}$$

**Step 3: From cellular to de Rham.** Singular homology with $\mathbb{R}$-coefficients is isomorphic to cellular homology (the cellular chain complex computes singular homology). By the de Rham theorem, there is a natural isomorphism
$$H^k_{\mathrm{dR}}(\mathbb{CP}^n) \cong H^k_{\mathrm{sing}}(\mathbb{CP}^n;\mathbb{R}).$$
Since $\mathbb{R}$ is a field, universal coefficients give
$$H^k_{\mathrm{sing}}(\mathbb{CP}^n;\mathbb{R}) \cong \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbb{R}}\!\big(H_k(\mathbb{CP}^n;\mathbb{R}),\,\mathbb{R}\big).$$
Combining with Step 2,
$$H^k_{\mathrm{dR}}(\mathbb{CP}^n) \cong \begin{cases} \mathbb{R}, & k \in \{0,2,4,\dots,2n\},\\ 0, & k \text{ odd or } k>2n.\end{cases}$$

**Step 4: Independent verification via the Kähler form (even degrees are nonzero).** To confirm the even-degree classes are genuinely nonzero (not merely formal consequences of the cell count), let $\omega$ be the Fubini–Study Kähler form on $\mathbb{CP}^n$. It is a closed real $2$-form. On each affine chart $U_j \cong \mathbb{C}^n$ with coordinates $w_1,\dots,w_n$,
$$\omega = \frac{i}{2\pi}\partial\bar\partial \log\!\Big(1+\sum_{\ell=1}^n |w_\ell|^2\Big),$$
which is a smooth closed $2$-form, so $[\omega] \in H^2_{\mathrm{dR}}(\mathbb{CP}^n)$.

A direct computation (using, e.g., the radial profile of $\omega$ in polar coordinates on each chart, or the fact that $\omega$ restricts to the standard volume form scaled appropriately) gives
$$\int_{\mathbb{CP}^1} \omega = 1,$$
where $\mathbb{CP}^1 \subset \mathbb{CP}^n$ is a projective line. Hence $[\omega]\ne 0$ in $H^2$.

The powers $\omega^j = \omega \wedge \cdots \wedge \omega$ ($j$ factors) are closed $2j$-forms. Moreover
$$\int_{\mathbb{CP}^j} \omega^j = \left(\int_{\mathbb{CP}^1}\omega\right)^j = 1 \ne 0,$$
where the last equality uses that $\omega$ restricts to the Fubini–Study form on each $\mathbb{CP}^j \subset \mathbb{CP}^n$ and that $\omega^j$ integrates over $\mathbb{CP}^j$ to the $j$-th power of the degree-$1$ integral over a line (this follows from the coarea / factorization of the volume form, or equivalently from $\int_{\mathbb{CP}^j}\omega^j = \mathrm{vol}(\mathbb{CP}^j)=1$ in the normalized Fubini–Study metric). Therefore $[\omega^j] \ne 0$ in $H^{2j}_{\mathrm{dR}}(\mathbb{CP}^n)$ for each $j=0,1,\dots,n$.

Since $\dim_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbb{CP}^n = 2n$, we have $H^k = 0$ for $k > 2n$. The classes $1, [\omega], [\omega^2], \dots, [\omega^n]$ provide one nonzero class in each even degree $0,2,\dots,2n$, matching the dimensions from Step 3. Thus each even-degree cohomology is exactly one-dimensional, spanned by $[\omega^j]$.

**Conclusion.**
$$\boxed{\,H^k_{\mathrm{dR}}(\mathbb{CP}^n) \cong \mathbb{R} \text{ for } k \in \{0,2,4,\dots,2n\}, \quad H^k_{\mathrm{dR}}(\mathbb{CP}^n) = 0 \text{ for } k \text{ odd or } k>2n.\,}$$

Equivalently, $H^*(\mathbb{CP}^n;\mathbb{R}) \cong \mathbb{R}[\omega]/(\omega^{n+1})$ with $\deg\omega = 2$.

